{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 6,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.104,
            0.138,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "150"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.782,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "MATHEMATICS"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.138,
            0.901,
            0.177
          ],
          "content": "Now AB is a chord of the circle $\\mathbf { C } _ { \\mathrm { i } }$ and $\\mathrm { O P \\bot A B }$ . Therefore, OP is the bisector of the chord AB, as the perpendicular from the centre bisects the chord,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.184,
            0.138,
            0.202
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.259,
            0.183,
            0.359,
            0.201
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm { A P } = \\mathrm { B P }",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.208,
            0.562,
            0.266
          ],
          "content": "Example 2 : Two tangents TP and TQ are drawn to a circle with centre O from an external point T. Prove that $\\angle PTQ = 2 \\angle OPQ$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.272,
            0.564,
            0.351
          ],
          "content": "Solution : We are given a circle with centre O, an external point T and two tangents TP and TQ to the circle, where P, Q are the points of contact (see Fig. 10.9). We need to prove that"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.602,
            0.224,
            0.884,
            0.361
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.395,
            0.365,
            0.595,
            0.385
          ],
          "content": "\\angle PTQ = 2 \\angle OPQ",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.708,
            0.368,
            0.807,
            0.388
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 10.9"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.401,
            0.14,
            0.418
          ],
          "content": "Let"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.395,
            0.399,
            0.517,
            0.42
          ],
          "content": "\\angle \\mathrm{PTQ} = \\theta",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.436,
            0.738,
            0.456
          ],
          "content": "Now, by Theorem 10.2, $\\mathrm { T P }   =   \\mathrm { T Q } .$ So, TPQ is an isosceles triangle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.481,
            0.206,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": "Therefore,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.3,
            0.468,
            0.738,
            0.508
          ],
          "content": "\\angle TPQ = \\angle TPP = \\frac{1}{2}(180^{\\circ} - \\theta) = 90^{\\circ} - \\frac{1}{2}\\theta",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.517,
            0.326,
            0.537
          ],
          "content": "Also, by Theorem 10.1,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.39,
            0.515,
            0.532,
            0.536
          ],
          "content": "\\angle  OPT = 90^{\\circ}",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.558,
            0.135,
            0.576
          ],
          "content": "So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.198,
            0.545,
            0.68,
            0.588
          ],
          "content": "\\angle OPQ = \\angle OPT - \\angle TPQ = 90^{\\circ} - \\left(90^{\\circ} - \\frac{1}{2}\\theta\\right)",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.497,
            0.601,
            0.655,
            0.643
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{1}{2} \\Theta = \\frac{1}{2} \\angle \\mathrm{PTQ}",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_19.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.652,
            0.201,
            0.671
          ],
          "content": "This gives"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.395,
            0.651,
            0.595,
            0.671
          ],
          "content": "\\angle PTQ = 2 \\angle OPQ",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_21.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.684,
            0.564,
            0.762
          ],
          "content": "Example 3 : PQ is a chord of length 8 cm of a circle of radius 5 cm. The tangents at P and Q intersect at a point T (see Fig. 10.10). Find the length TP."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.766,
            0.563,
            0.863
          ],
          "content": "Solution : Join OT. Let it intersect PQ at the point R. Then ∆ TPQ is isosceles and TO is the angle bisector of $\\angle   \\mathrm { { \\Large ~ P T Q } }$ . So, OT ⊥ PQ and therefore, OT bisects PQ which gives $\\mathrm{PR} = \\mathrm{RQ} = 4\\mathrm{cm}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.569,
            0.67,
            0.901,
            0.863
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.695,
                0.87,
                0.807,
                0.89
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 10.10"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_24.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.875,
            0.585,
            0.902
          ],
          "content": "Also, $\\mathrm{OR}=\\sqrt{\\mathrm{OP}^{2}-\\mathrm{PR}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5^{2}-4^{2}} \\mathrm{cm}=3 \\mathrm{cm}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.966,
            0.563,
            0.983
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
